1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A grammar says how to build strings. Nothing in it promises to build each string in one way only.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The expression grammar E goes to E plus E, or E times E, or the operand i, builds i plus i times i in two ways.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
The two ways are i plus i, all times i, and i plus, i times i. Different trees, different arithmetic.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
That is ambiguity of a grammar: one string, two parse trees. It is a property of the rules you were handed.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The second question is about the language: is there any grammar for these strings with one tree each?

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
A parse tree has the start symbol at the root, one node per rule used, and the string along the bottom.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A grammar is ambiguous when some string in its language has two different parse trees.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Equivalently, two different leftmost derivations: always expand the leftmost nonterminal next.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Not two different derivations. Expanding the right branch first is the same tree written in another order.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
So ambiguity is about the tree, never about the order in which the steps are written down.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Deciding one string is mechanical. Fill one cell for each piece of the string, shortest pieces first.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The cell for a piece counts the trees that build it from E. A piece that is one letter holds 1.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
For i plus i plus i the six pieces hold 1, 1, 1, 1, 1 and then 2 for the whole string.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Two is the answer: the string has two trees, so this grammar is ambiguous. Nothing was guessed.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The work is cubic in the length, so a machine settles one string quickly and a person should not try by hand.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Count the operands and the answer is a Catalan number: 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42 for one operand to six.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Two operands cannot be ambiguous here; three are enough. The shortest witness is i plus i plus i.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
With a multiplication in the string the two trees also disagree about the value: eight against six when i is 2.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
The count belongs to the rules, not to the strings. Another grammar for the same strings counts differently.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Six operands already give 42 trees, so the growth is fast and a hand count stops being a proof.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
The repair splits one nonterminal into three levels: E for plus, T for times, F for an operand.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
E goes to E plus T or T, T goes to T times F or F, F goes to id. Each string now has one tree.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Computed: the six counts fall from 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42 to 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1.

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The language did not change: 363 strings tested, none changed sides, and no levelled count went above 1.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Precedence and associativity are decisions the grammar makes. Leaving them out is what created the ambiguity.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
The dangling else is the same failure in a language: if c then if c then x else x.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
The else can belong to the inner if or to the outer one. Both trees are legal, so the grammar is ambiguous.

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The textbook repair splits statements into matched and unmatched, so an else always closes the nearest if.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
A repair that lets the else branch be any statement stays ambiguous: computed, the same string still has 2 trees.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The version whose else branch is unmatched gives 1, and 782 tested strings agree on the language.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Three different numbers hide behind the word derivation. Keep them apart and the definition stops wobbling.

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
For i plus i: one tree, one leftmost derivation, and two derivation sequences.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
For i plus i plus i: two trees, two leftmost derivations, and sixteen sequences.

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
So "two derivations" is a test only if you mean leftmost, or if you count trees. Otherwise it overcounts.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
A grammar with many derivation orders and one tree per string is unambiguous, and Knuth says so about his own.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Now the harder question: is the language itself ambiguous, whatever grammar anybody writes for it?

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Take strings a to the i, b to the j, c to the k with i equal to j, or j equal to k. Two families, one language.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The overlap strings a to the n b to the n c to the n belong to both families, so they get 2 trees.

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
So this grammar is ambiguous on infinitely many strings and on nothing else. That is still ambiguity.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Ginsburg and Ullian proved in 1966 that no grammar for this language escapes it. That is inherent ambiguity.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Why can no program decide ambiguity? Because a decider would also decide Post's correspondence problem.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
From the dominoes build one grammar: S goes to U or to V, and U spells the top row while V spells the bottom.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
A matching sequence makes one string that both halves generate, so that string has exactly 2 trees.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Computed: with a match, 2 strings sit in both halves and carry 2 trees each; with no match, 0 and never more than 1.

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
So a decider for ambiguity would answer the domino question, which is undecidable. Cantor, Floyd, Chomsky and Schützenberger, 1962 to 1963.

46
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
So: ambiguity of a string is decidable, ambiguity of a grammar is not, inherent ambiguity of a language is not.

47
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
A regular grammar is decidable and always repairable: 510 strings tested, never more than 1 tree.

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
A grammar can even be infinitely ambiguous: S to S S, or a, or epsilon gives the empty string infinitely many trees.

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Four exam shapes: which grammar is ambiguous, how many trees, is the language inherently ambiguous, what is decidable.

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The line to carry: name the object first — string, grammar, or language — and the answer follows.

